  "scriptId": "pon-09-three-numbers-pairwise-sums",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Three Numbers and Pairwise-Sum Puzzles",
    "description": "Recover three unknown numbers from pairwise sums, chained ratios, linked equations and symmetric identities.",
    "teachingApproach": "Open with the elegant pairwise-sum total trick, then broaden to total-plus-multiple relations, chained ratios, linked substitutions, the three-number square identity, pairwise averages and direct recovery from pairwise sums. The capstone combines a pair-sum difference with a ratio, so the learner must reuse two earlier ideas rather than merely repeat one formula.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "Teach pairwise sums x+y, y+z and x+z by adding all three to get 2(x+y+z), then subtracting the opposite pair to recover each number. Extend to chained ratios A:B and B:C, a total with one number a multiple or fraction of another, and linked linear conditions such as x+y, y+z and 3x+z. Use (a+b+c)^2=a^2+b^2+c^2+2(ab+bc+ca) when the chapter gives sums of squares and pairwise products. Include average-of-pairs wording as another form of pairwise sums. End with a capstone that combines a pairwise total with a ratio relation."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's solve three-number puzzles without juggling three equations blindly.\n\nThe chapter often gives overlapping clues. The fastest move is to notice what cancels when those clues are added or subtracted.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Three pairwise sums hide a neat shortcut.\n\nSuppose:\nx+y=12\ny+z=17\nx+z=19",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Add all three equations:\n2x+2y+2z = 48\nSo x+y+z = 24.\n\nNow subtract the pair that does not contain the number you want:\nx = 24−(y+z)=24−17=7\nSimilarly y=5,z=12.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
